WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.500
Film Courage: Going back to your first film
WHY MEN CHEAT in 2005, so how did that movie's

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.920
success impact you getting your next film
project off the ground?

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.770
Mark Harris, Filmmaker: Yes, so it was a while.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:16.640
My first time that I ever got distribution
for was a film called BLACK BUTTERFLY.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.320
it was a film we made for less than $10,000.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:25.060
And E1 actually put that film out (eOne is
a major company).

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.390
A guy named Allen Blackwell saw the trailer.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.149
I sent him the trailer and he said “I want
this film.”

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:40.940
Then he did my first film WHY MEN CHEAT, I
USED TO LOVE HER, HOLLA IF YOU HEAR ME.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:47.350
Those films from WHY MEN CHEAT we took that
money and we invested it HOLLA IF YOU HEAR

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:48.350
ME.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.360
We took that money and invested it into I
USED TO LOVE HER.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.969
And I USED TO LOVE HER, we did that film for
like $3,500.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:59.579
And it was in like 10 film festivals.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:03.430
When I went to the film festivals I didn’t
just go there just to screen the film.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:08.080
When I would go there I would have DVDs made
up.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.010
Honestly I wasn’t even thinking about distribution.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.650
I was just thinking about selling DVDs.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:25.360
And it wasn’t until BLACK BUTTERFLY is when
I said “Okay, if I have to release this

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.890
film on my own then I will do that.”

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:38.390
What we did was I sent the trailer to Allen
Blackwell at eOne.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.220
He said “I want that film.”

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:45.390
And then from that film we got distribution
for HOLLA IF YOU HEAR ME.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:54.900
Because of the success of that film, with
BLACK BUTTERFLY…eOne the first week had

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.899
to reprint more DVDs because they had sold
out in the stores (in Walmart and they were

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:01.920
all sold out).

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.100
So they had to rush and print more.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.750
I told them “Look I have an audience out
there and what we want to do is we’re going

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.690
to promote this film so you are going to have
to print up more.”

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.850
I think they printed up 5,000 copies the first
time which I understand they want to be safe

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.640
and so the first week or so those were all
gone.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.950
Because of the success of BLACK BUTTERFLY
is when we got distribution for I USED TO

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:33.180
LOVE HER, then we got distribution for HOLLA
IF YOU HIT ME from the success of that film.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.480
Film Courage: And so you made it for $10,000?

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.710
The one that was in Walmart?

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.569
Mark: Yes, it was for like $9,500.

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:45.480
Film Courage: How many locations did you have?

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.150
Mark: We had a total of I think eight locations.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.370
But this is how we would do it.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:59.320
We would shoot on weekends because people
worked, so we would shoot on weekends.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:05.570
We would shoot a 12-hour day on Saturday and
Sunday.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:11.500
On Friday what we would do is take one small
scene and we would just shoot that scene and

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.230
that’s how we got the film made.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.010
It was like 6 weeks.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.450
Film Courage: So that’s how you were able
to keep it [the budget] so low?

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.740
Mark: That’s how we were able to keep it
so low.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:29.569
And also…here’s the secret…the secret
is you have to work with…I know it’s difficult

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:35.330
in LA because when you talk about locations
because people are familiar with how much

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.010
money you pay for locations.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.240
But in Chicago it’s pretty different.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:45.610
So in Chicago for us it’s that a lot of
the film we shot at my mom’s house.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:53.390
I shot some of the film at my auntie’s house
and a lot of exterior stuff where we didn’t

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:54.750
need permits to set up.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.640
We would go to a park and shoot.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.680
Like the park that we shot at is the park
that I grew up playing basketball and baseball

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.680
and things like that.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.080
So we would go to that park and we would shoot
things.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.400
We didn’t pay a lot of money for locations
at all because we would use friends and family’s

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.400
locations.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.139
That’s another thing you should do, just
look at your friends, ask your friends as

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:20.409
far as can you shoot at their home?

00:04:20.409 --> 00:04:25.749
Say for instance if you shoot at someone’s
home, just be respectful of their home.

00:04:25.749 --> 00:04:32.009
Maybe donate $100 or $150 toward electricity.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:35.370
I know in LA it’s kind of expensive (locations
are expensive).

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:42.159
But if you are shooting somewhere else just
ask the person if you can shoot there.

00:04:42.159 --> 00:04:42.389
And they are open to it.

